[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding GraphQL: Execution Engine and AST"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"When a GraphQL query is sent to a server, it undergoes a rigorous lifecycle before returning data to the client. The two core components responsible for parsing and fulfilling this query are the Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) and the Execution Engine."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"The Role of the Abstract Syntax Tree (AST)"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The first phase of processing a GraphQL request involves the Lexical Analysis (Tokenization) and Syntactic Analysis (Parsing). The parser reads the raw string query and transforms it into an AST, which is a hierarchical tree representation of the query structure."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"query GetUser {
  user(id: "1") {
    name
    email
  }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"The code above is parsed by the GraphQL parser into an AST document node containing operation definitions, field nodes, and argument nodes. This structure allows the server to validate the query against the schema before execution."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Validation and Analysis"},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Type Checking: Ensures that requested fields exist on the target types."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Argument Validation: Confirms that required arguments are provided and match the expected scalar types."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Directive Verification: Evaluates directives like @include or @skip to prune branches of the AST."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"The Execution Engine"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once the AST is validated, it is handed over to the Execution Engine. The engine traverses the AST breadth-first or depth-first, invoking resolver functions associated with each field in the schema."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"const resolvers = {
  Query: {
    user(parent, args, context, info) {
      return context.db.getUserById(args.id);
    }
  },
  User: {
    name(parent) {
      return parent.fullName;
    }
  }
};"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the example above, the execution engine calls the Query.user resolver first. The resulting object becomes the parent (or source) for the subsequent field resolvers, such as User.name and User.email."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Handling Asynchronous Resolvers and DataLoaders"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"GraphQL execution handles asynchronous resolvers naturally by returning Promises. However, querying lists of data can lead to the N+1 query problem. To optimize execution, engines often utilize batching and caching mechanisms like DataLoader."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Resolvers return Promises or direct values."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The engine waits for all sibling fields to resolve before constructing the final JSON response payload."},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"By understanding how the AST validates your queries and how the Execution Engine orchestrates your resolvers, you can write more efficient schemas and optimized resolver logic."}]
